@H@T0SED is a non interactive batch stream editorSyntax: sed [<opts>] [<path>] [<opts>]        sed [-n] <script> [<path>]Function: SED reads a text file from <path>, edits it and writes it to           <stdout>. If no path is given, SED reads from <stdin>. The editing          commands are given by the string <script> or by the file <sfile>.Options:    -?              display usage            -n              no output            -e=<script>     editing commands            -f=<sfile>      file with editing commandsIf there is only one script string the preceeding "-e=" may be omitted.There may be more than one -e or -f option. @D1 UScript : 2Commands : 3Regular_Expressions : 4Edition_Notes : 5@T2A script consists of editing commands each written on a seperate line. Such a line has the following general format:        [address [,address]] command [arguments]SED reads the input file (or <stdin>) line by line and copies each line intoa search buffer. Then SED uses all given script commands which have a matchingaddress on this buffer. Finally SED outputs the buffer to <stdout> except forthe -n option set.If <address> matches to the line stored into the search buffer the <command> isused on this line. <address> may be one of the following:    a decimal number        <command> is used on the line with the given line                            number    a "$" sign              <command> is used on the last line    a /regular expression/  <command> is used if the regular expression matches                            the current lineA command without a given address will be used on all input lines.Two addresses seperated by a "," select a range of lines to which the commandwill be used on.There is a second buffer called save buffer for some special commands.@T3The following commands are valid for SED:    a\    text        append. Output <text> before reading next input line.        b label     branch to the ":" command which is marked by <label>. If no                 label is given SED branches to the end of the script.        c\    text        change. Delete search buffer. If no or one address is given or                at the end of a range given by two addresses <text> will be                 output.        d           Delete search buffer and read next line.        D           Delete search buffer area up to the next newline character.        g           Substitute the contents of the search buffer with the contents                of the save buffer.        G           Append the contents of the save buffer to the contents of the                search buffer.        h           Substitute the contents of the save buffer with the contents                of the search buffer.        H           Append the contents of the search buffer ti the contents of                the save buffer.        i\    text        insert. Output <text>        n           Output search buffer and read next input line.        N           Append next input line to the search buffer. The newline                 character will not be removed. The current line number will be                incremented.        p           Output the contents of the search buffer.        P           Output the contents of the search buffer up to the first                 newline character.        r rfile     Read <rfile> and output it before reading the next input line.        s /regular expression/replacement/flags                substitute. A matching <regular expression> in the search buffer                will be substituted by <replacement>.                flags:  g       global. Substitute all non overlaping occurences                                of <regular expression> instead of substitute                                the first occurence only.                        p       print. Output the pattern area if a                                 substitution has taken place.                        w file  write. Append the pattern area to <file> if a                                substitution has taken place.     t label     test. Branch to the next ":" command with the given <label> if                there was at least one substitution since the last reading of an                inputl line or since the last "t" command. If <label> is not                 given it will be branched to the end of the script.    w file      write. Append search buffer to <file>.        x           Exchange the contents of the search buffer for the contents of                the save buffer.        y /string1/string2/                transform. Change all occurences of characters given by                string1 into the corresponding characters given by string2.                ! command   negate. <command> is used on lines not matching the preceeding                address.      : label     This command does nothing but marks a destination for a branch                by the b or t command.                =           Output the current line number.        {}          The enclosed commands are grouped together and are executed if                the preceeding address matches to the contents of the search                buffer.A <text> argument may be consists of several lines. If a line ends with a "\"character it continues in the following line.@T4A regular expression is used to define a search pattern to be compared with atext string. It consists of normal characters (operands) and meta-characters(operands).The regular expression meta-characters and their use are:    \   The escape character introduces an escape sequence:            \b      Backspace            \t      TAB-character            \n      newline character            \l      line feed character            \f      form feed character            \ddd    octal value            \c      c can be any character that is taken literally                ^   The carat matches the beginning of a string.        When "^" is the first character in a character class, the character        denotes a negated character class.        $   The dollar sign matches the end of a string.        .   The period matches any character.        [   The open bracket denotes the beginning of a character class.        ]   The closed bracked denotes the end of a character class.        |   This character is the alternation character.    ()  Parantheses are used to group expressions in much the same manner in        that they are used with arithmetic expressions.            *   The closure operator matches 0 or more of the specified expression.        +   The plus sign indicates positive closure and matches 1 or more of the        specified expression.            ?   The question mark matches 0 or 1 of the specified expression.    Character classes are "shorthand" for matching one of several characters. Forexample, [AaBb] is the same as (A|a|B|b) and matches "A", "a", "B" or "b".There also character ranges, such as [A-Z], which match any uppercase alpha character. Negated character classes act in the opposite way, and they specifycharacters that should not be matched. For example, [^A-Z] matches everything except uppercase alpha characters.Examples:"^[a-z]$"    matches any line that consists of only lowercase letters."[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]"    matches a C variable anywhere on a line."(soft|loud) (classical|rock|country) music"    matches six strings from "soft classical music" to "loud country music"."^[0-9].*[^0-9]$"    matches any line that begins with a digit and doesn't end in a digit."80[123]?8[86] | 680[01234]0"    matches any of the more popular microprocessors, plus a few thet never    made it.@T5The source of SED was originally written by GNU.SED has been adapted to OS-9/68k by         Hubert Nehring        Dahlemer Str. 4        D-5090 Leverkusen 1The adaption has been carried out by:-   changing the option parsing method in order to be compatible with the    OS-9 option convention-   adding a -? option-   using register variables extensively in order to minimize the size of the    resulting program and to maximize its execution speed.    